1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,567
Imagine finding the course you need. Registration is open. You have made your choice. But the last

2
00:00:06,567 --> 00:00:13,133
button never receives keyboard focus. You should not have to ask someone else to finish. Can I

3
00:00:13,133 --> 00:00:18,155
Finish helps developers reproduce that barrier, inspect the evidence, and check their repair.

4
00:00:18,155 --> 00:00:25,798
First, a real public website. The task is to find the iPhone VoiceOver setup guide from Apple's

5
00:00:25,798 --> 00:00:33,441
accessibility page, using only the keyboard. The runner opens a fresh browser and follows an explicit task

6
00:00:33,441 --> 00:00:39,735
contract. These are real browser recordings. Repetitive navigation has been condensed for the demonstration.

7
00:00:39,735 --> 00:00:46,129
The runner reaches the VoiceOver details, activates the iPhone setup link, and verifies the heading on the

8
00:00:46,129 --> 00:00:52,522
actual support article. The captured run passed in thirty nine key events. That is evidence about this

9
00:00:52,522 --> 00:00:58,540
one journey, in this browser. It is not a claim that the whole website is accessible.

10
00:00:58,540 --> 00:01:05,433
Every key has a screenshot, a focus record, and a timestamp. You can inspect the task contract

11
00:01:05,433 --> 00:01:12,327
and replay the exact point where progress stopped. The original browser recording is also preserved. This turns

12
00:01:12,327 --> 00:01:19,220
an unclear report, such as I cannot get through, into a concrete sequence another developer can investigate.

13
00:01:19,220 --> 00:01:26,327
Now the registration barrier from our opening example. This is our deliberately broken demonstration page. The control

14
00:01:26,327 --> 00:01:33,434
responds to a mouse click, but is missing from sequential keyboard focus. After eighteen Tab presses, the

15
00:01:33,434 --> 00:01:40,542
runner stops with a blocked result. The screenshot and target markup explain what was observed, without pretending

16
00:01:40,542 --> 00:01:43,050
every failure has the same cause.

17
00:01:43,050 --> 00:01:50,338
Here the demonstration replaces that control with a native button. The identical contract now reaches registration in

18
00:01:50,338 --> 00:01:57,627
five key events. The comparison keeps the task constant while the implementation changes. That matters because a

19
00:01:57,627 --> 00:02:04,915
suggested fix is only a hypothesis until the task has been exercised again and the finish line

20
00:02:04,915 --> 00:02:06,630
has actually been reached.

21
00:02:06,630 --> 00:02:13,856
A second example checks a dialog. In the broken version, Escape leaves it open, and the diagnostic

22
00:02:13,856 --> 00:02:21,083
keys remain inside. In the corrected version, Escape closes the dialog, restores focus, and lets registration continue.

23
00:02:21,083 --> 00:02:28,309
The issue is the failed exit and task continuation, rather than the normal focus containment that a

24
00:02:28,309 --> 00:02:30,860
modal dialog is supposed to provide.

25
00:02:30,860 --> 00:02:37,746
Finally, export the evidence and an executable keyboard regression test. Run it again after the next change.

26
00:02:37,746 --> 00:02:44,633
The browser runner is free and local, and the hosted viewer makes its captured evidence easy to

27
00:02:44,633 --> 00:02:51,519
inspect. Can I Finish is a prototype, not a full accessibility audit. Its purpose is practical: help

28
00:02:51,519 --> 00:02:55,165
teams make sure people can finish ordinary tasks independently.